1. TIMELINE MERGING (How Branches Combine Behind the Scenes)
------------------------------------------------------------------------
When you click Merge on GitHub, Git automatically glues your branch 
back into the master line using one of two smooth methods:

* Fast-Forward Merge: 
  -> Use case: No one else touched the project while you were working.
  -> Simply put: Git doesn't do any complex math. It just slides the 
     "main" baseline marker forward to match your latest commit. 
     Your timeline stays perfectly straight and clean.

* Three-Way Merge: 
  -> Use case: A teammate pushed a code fix while you were working.
  -> Simply put: Your timelines officially split into a "Y" shape. 
     Git runs a script to combine both paths and automatically creates 
     a brand-new "Merge Commit" to sew the two timelines back into one.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. SEMANTIC COMMITS (Writing Git Logs Like a Professional)
------------------------------------------------------------------------
DevOps teams never write sloppy commit messages like "fixed stuff". 
Always use standard industry prefixes so automated tools can scan 
your logs and write server update notes automatically. 

Always type a lowercase prefix followed by a colon:

* feat: (New Feature / Infrastructure)
  -> Example: "feat: add AWS S3 storage bucket configuration"

* fix: (Patching a Bug / Repairing a Link)
  -> Example: "fix: update broken cloud database port routing"

* docs: (Editing Documentation / Study Files)
  -> Example: "docs: update operations guide with PAT token links"

* ci: (Modifying Automation Scripts / Pipelines)
  -> Example: "ci: update github actions deployment workflow text"

